Investigation evidence chain — SideCamera AC-4 frame timing

Scope: only one-camera AC-4 capture/publication cadence. Build source remains unchanged. All times below are UTC unless noted. The `Observed` rows are direct file or command observations; the final mechanism statement is inference and does not count as another observed link.

1. Observed — repeated current-source sidecamerad capture (2026-11-15 08:15:26–08:20:31 UTC). The assigned front-left by-path resolved to `/dev/video60`. It published 6,050 selected matched frames over 302.506666 s, with 6,049 EOF intervals averaging 49.998692 ms (20.000523 fps), maximum 89.163715 ms, and 753 intervals above 60 ms. Frame IDs were contiguous and cereal/VisionIPC timestamps identical. Inputs matched the build package's nine recorded source hashes before and after. Source captures: `../build/evidence/runtime-smoke-ac4-r2.2-round2.log` SHA-256 `de804d35326c6925862d2c8b36708d4a14a10b05120076924f523397976e8c55`; `../build/artifacts/runtime_probe-ac4-r2.2-round2.json` SHA-256 `8a4a6d1d93b83687630b227c78f16177e3159333dfe97e29fdd5a8410c51b9f4`; `../build/artifacts/runtime_cadence_intervals-r2.2-round2.json` SHA-256 `967b422432e1cf8f2f2fc96bbead768574448a850c51245835325de63939f8a5`; report `../build/reports/report_runtime_health-ac4-r2.2-round2.md` SHA-256 `2200d7460f60c1188a68ffa5d79f53a70a137d7d55d00e5a49d3d66874a088f1`.

2. Observed — subscriber behavior in the same sidecamerad run. VisionIPC receive intervals averaged 49.998402 ms, maximum 89.389179 ms, with 747 of 6,049 over 60 ms. Median receive lag behind producer EOF was 0.881836 ms (maximum 7.017679 ms). The reader series followed the producer series; there was no queue divergence, sequence gap, or timestamp mismatch. Source: the health report and raw per-frame interval capture named above.

3. Observed — direct V4L2 capture without FFmpeg or sidecamerad (log saved 2026-11-15 08:31:24 UTC; elapsed wall 60.22 s including device startup). Read-only format query reported 1280x720 MJPG at 20 fps. `/usr/bin/v4l2-ctl` streamed 1,200 mmap buffers to `/dev/null` (`--stream-mmap=4 --stream-count=1200 --stream-to=/dev/null --stream-poll --verbose --stream-show-delta-now`). The kernel timestamp source was monotonic, `ts-src-soe`; sequence numbers ran 0–1199 without gaps. Excluding the one 167.987 ms initial startup interval, 1,198 intervals averaged 49.995553 ms, ranged 47.672–52.287 ms, and none exceeded 60 ms. No pixel data was written. Capture: `evidence/v4l2-capture-timestamps-60s.log` SHA-256 `bf3ea3aee6ea62d9b3b7010db2ed89e6c4755a536eb2202c0790ae8f72b21999`.

4. Observed — FFmpeg-to-raw-pipe completion timing without sidecamerad IPC (2026-11-15 08:36:26–08:37:22 UTC). A Python `poll`/`readv` loop ran the same FFmpeg V4L2/MJPEG to NV12 rawvideo command for 1,090 complete 1,382,400-byte frames, discarded the pixels, and recorded monotonic timestamps only after each full frame read. Over 55.890414 s, 1,089 intervals averaged 49.991474 ms (20.003411 fps), ranged 10.275–88.842 ms; 133 exceeded 60 ms and 147 were below 40 ms. All nine package source hashes matched before and after. No cereal, VisionIPC, Params, frame publisher, or recording file was involved. CPU pressure was nonzero but modest (`some avg10` 2.98% before and 3.38% after); system load averages were 1.37 then 2.25 and cannot attribute per-process scheduling. FFmpeg did not exit within the 1.0 s SIGTERM wait after the target frames, so the controller escalated to SIGKILL and reaped it; no child remained. Captures: `evidence/ffmpeg-pipe-timing-60s.log` SHA-256 `5454846524f28064d3ed5e1ae8aafb39f9e2fb6fbc33a87bdcafe4cacc5c850e`; `artifacts/ffmpeg-pipe-timing-60s.json` SHA-256 `50a969cb540f1c6abd365f69d0af97934621cdc37849ce48de0c62277738d155`.

5. Observed — source timestamp assignment (source inspected 2026-11-15 08:39 UTC). `system/sidecamerad.py` reads exactly `FRAME_BYTES`, then calls `_publish(time.monotonic_ns())` at line 198. The FFmpeg command outputs rawvideo bytes on stdout; the current worker does not consume a per-frame camera capture timestamp. The source file hash at inspection is `494fb8b88fda8bcf1d8301225019f4f01f4b77a0bb5cb3e69dde47ed96e1309b`.

6. Observed — earlier five-minute failure is consistent: 6,000 matching IDs; 19.999396661241942 fps mean; 123.185415 ms maximum gap; 743 of 5,999 intervals above 60 ms. Capture: `../build/evidence/runtime-smoke-zmq-r2-final.log` SHA-256 `899ff2ff4758ce4647b03d90436866a6ea5eebc95cbca65b8de57872b32930dd`.

Inference (medium confidence): the recorded EOF jitter is introduced after regular V4L2 capture timestamps and before IPC publication, in the FFmpeg raw-output / pipe-read completion path. The no-IPC reproduction rules out VisionIPC or cereal publishing and subscriber backlog as the source of the >60 ms intervals. The direct V4L2 sample makes irregular sensor/driver cadence unsupported for this device/run, but it is a separate sequential capture rather than a simultaneous trace. Current evidence does not distinguish FFmpeg decode/output timing from host scheduling of FFmpeg/Python pipe work.

Hypotheses displaced: IPC publisher or reader queue backlog (observed no-IPC reproduction still jitters; subscriber lag remains low); frame loss or reorder (IDs contiguous); source input hash drift (all nine hashes stayed identical); V4L2 kernel capture cadence as the likely cause (direct source timestamps were regular).

Hypotheses not distinguished: FFmpeg decode/output timing versus host/Python scheduling around pipe delivery and full-frame reads. The rawvideo byte stream contains no consumed per-frame source timestamp in the current implementation, and the diagnostic did not measure per-process scheduler delay or correlate the same V4L2 buffer timestamp with each decoded output frame.
